Slide 7 – Phase 3: Silver News Processing and Financial Sentiment Analysis

Time: 2 minutes

Phase 3 focuses on processing the raw news records stored in the Bronze layer.

The main objective is to convert raw article information into clean, validated, company-specific data with sentiment scores.

The overall process is orchestrated by "run_silver_pipeline.py".

Let us understand the processing steps.

First, reading the Bronze data.

The "read_bronze.py" component discovers and reads the new Bronze Parquet files.

It extracts the raw JSON content and preserves the source file information for traceability.

Second, checkpoint management.

The "checkpoint_manager.py" component maintains two checkpoint files: "processed_files.json" and "processed_article_ids.json".

The first tracks which Bronze files have already been processed.

The second tracks article IDs that have already been handled.

This prevents unnecessary reprocessing during subsequent pipeline executions.

Third, deduplication.

The "deduplicate_news.py" component removes previously processed articles and duplicates within the current batch.

This ensures that each unique article is processed only once.

Fourth, validation.

The "validate_news.py" component checks the required fields and validates the JSON records.

Records that fail validation are sent to the dead-letter output along with the reason for rejection.

Fifth, text cleaning.

The "clean_news.py" component uses BeautifulSoup to remove HTML tags and entities and normalize the available text.

This prepares the article text for further processing.

Sixth, ticker extraction.

The "extract_tickers.py" component uses the "company_aliases.json" configuration file.

It identifies company names and their aliases in the news content and maps them to their canonical NSE ticker symbols.

This step is important because the Gold layer needs a consistent ticker to correlate news records with stock records.

Seventh, sentiment analysis.

For sentiment analysis, we use the pretrained "ProsusAI FinBERT" model.

FinBERT is a language model designed for financial text.

The text is first passed through the tokenizer, which converts it into tokens and model-compatible input representations.

The model then performs inference and generates three sentiment probabilities: positive, negative, and neutral.

These probabilities help us understand the sentiment expressed in each news article.

We process the articles in batches of eight.

An important optimization is that sentiment is calculated once per unique article. If an article is associated with multiple companies, the same sentiment result is reused for its different ticker mappings.

Finally, the processed data is written into three outputs.

The "silver/news" output stores one record per unique article.

The "silver/news_by_ticker" output stores one record for each article-and-ticker combination.

The "dead_letter" output stores invalid or rejected records with their rejection reasons.

For safe output writing, we use temporary staging and commit handling. The checkpoints are updated only after the output has been successfully committed.

This is important because it reduces the risk of marking records as processed before their output has actually been saved.

In our test execution, two Bronze Parquet files containing 14 records were processed.

All 14 records were valid, and all 14 articles had ticker matches.

These produced 16 article-ticker mappings.

During the second execution, the pipeline skipped the already processed files and did not repeat the sentiment inference.

This confirms that the checkpoint and deduplication mechanisms are working as expected.

